registry projects to `opaque_event` with `opaque` visibility, keeps a Source Evidence whose raw blob/manifest byte-range references actually resolve back to the captured bytes (sorted, unique, in range), and is never dropped, guessed, coerced into a neighbouring kind or re-typed on a second projection (project twice and compare). Drive a record that is ALMOST a known kind (right envelope, unknown inner type) as well as a wholly unknown one.\n4|(2) Foreign signed/encrypted reasoning stays opaque. A foreign encrypted or signed reasoning record projects to `opaque_reasoning`, is preserved byte-exact (its bytes survive a round trip through the blob store and back), is never decrypted, re-encoded, summarized, truncated or promoted to `reasoning_summary`, and never becomes higher-authority content. Foreign instructions remain LOW-AUTHORITY history: prove that an instruction carried in a foreign record cannot change the projected session's effective instruction snapshot or authority, and that source usage numbers never become target accounting.\n5|(3) Incomplete tool calls never become live actions. Historical tools are inert: a `tool_definition_snapshot` projects as history and cannot register a callable tool; a `tool_call` without its `tool_result` becomes ABORTED history and blocks pending action — drive the whole matrix (call with result; call without result; result without call; call whose result arrives after the capture boundary; nested/subagent call) and assert the projected kind, the visibility and the \"no pending action\" property for each. A mutant that lets an incomplete call project as pending or callable must redden a test.\n6|(4) The closed shapes that carry all of the above: Canonical Event's exact member set (zero-based contiguous ordinal, 0..64 sorted unique causal parents, Actor UUIDv7, nullable turn, kind from the closed 26-kind registry, nullable source time, public|projection|internal|opaque visibility, kind-selected closed payload, Source Evidence, extensions); Source Evidence with capture status exact|partial|synthesized|unavailable and stable reason codes, where a SYNTHESIZED event has no native Event ID and identifies the core operation that made it; message-like payloads using exactly the closed content blocks text|json|image|audio|document|resource_link|redacted|opaque with typed inline content or Blob Descriptor references and inline content at most 64 KiB (witness 65536 accepted and 65537 refused, and prove that oversized inline content becomes a blob reference rather than a silent truncation); Canonical Session bounds 1..1024 Actors, 1..1,000,000 ordered unique Event IDs, 1..1024 head IDs, an Actor's `parent` null only for `main`.\n7|Evidence: every row driven through a production entry with a named committed test, an n-of-m ratio and call sites; a projection determinism proof (same captured bytes -> byte-identical canonical output twice); negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control; and a conformance matrix over every 13.14.1/10.2/7.8 clause this Story claims, mapped to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with its owner. State explicitly any part of 13.14.2-13.14.5 (fidelity/lineage, bundle chain, transaction, events and tuple admission) that this Story does NOT own, so the matrix cannot be read as a wider claim.\n8|COMMON CONTRACT (FINAL leaf of its Story — your Change Request is the story_final whole-Story patch). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named above; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260917-110onn) may land on trunk during your run — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers and use the v0.7.0 artifacts everywhere the v0.6.0 ones are named below. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) and above all the packages your own Story's earlier leaves added on this branch — never fork a second model of anything already modeled; extend or adopt and say which. STORY-CLOSE ITEMS, all owned by this leaf: (a) internal/traceability ownership registry bindings for every normative section this Story claims plus the Story's acceptance case bound to the REAL conformance suite with honest coverage text that separates executed lanes from stated bounds (precedent Stories 3drr2m/3jqsx1/3m2mw8/2jylym/1kiyj6) — edit the ADOPTED registry, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` if the v0.7.0 adoption is on trunk when you refresh, otherwise `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.6.0.json`, and never invent a binding for a clause no test drives; (b) re-derive and re-pin `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` in internal/traceability/traceability.go from the semantic JSON projection of the registry you just edited — derive it, never copy a digest from another Story, and show the derivation command and output in the results; (c) `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` green plus the section-scoped run for each section you bound, with the measured coverage ratio quoted verbatim in the results; (d) README: a section for each package this Story added with its exact test commands and NO capability or CLI claim (no `ax` command exists), and the \"Measured coverage of this repository\" subsection updated to the numbers tracecheck actually prints; (e) one newest-first LOGBOOK entry for the Story. The candidate's task-board.config.json must stay byte-identical to trunk unless you deliberately add a bounded validation command — if you add one, say so explicitly in the results and keep it fast. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write or an explicit purity bound; a conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with an owner. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1cyj0q ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is the previous leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision, rebase, commit on, amend or revert it, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint; the CR the runtime builds from it is the WHOLE-Story patch, so read every earlier leaf's delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree log --stat <trunk>..HEAD`) and its outcome resources before you start. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-32ypr2` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD; the ownership registry must MERGE the trunk rows with yours, never replace them). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured validation suite (30 commands as of trunk c3aae73 — STORY-260830-4qojoz added three bounded rpcwire fuzz gates; read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it) once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-32ypr2_results.md, TASK-260830-32ypr2_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-32ypr2_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-32ypr2 --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n9|\n10|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n11|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n12|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n13|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n14|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n15|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n16|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n17|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n18|\n19|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n20|\n21|YOUR PREVIOUS ATTEMPT FAILED AT CR CONSTRUCTION FOR A PROVENANCE REASON, NOT FOR ANYTHING IN YOUR WORK — READ THIS FIRST. The construction refused with:\n22|`delivery failure [stale-anchor]: change_request_base_authority_mismatch: the STORY-260830-1cyj0q candidate provenance disagrees: checkpoint 47294bb8 does not descend from selected authority c3aae73 while branch=47294bb8 and head=47294bb8`.\n23|Nothing about that is your content. The Story branch was cut before trunk advanced: its tip is the TASK-260830-2g5be6 checkpoint sitting on base a12d1bd, while current origin/main is c3aae73 (PR #54 landed STORY-260830-4qojoz, which also raised the configured suite from 27 to 30 commands). A Change Request must descend from the selected authority, so the branch has to move onto c3aae73 before any CR can be constructed here.\n24|So, BEFORE you do anything else: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-32ypr2`. You are the live tracked producer for this initial leaf and it provably has no Change Request, which is exactly the case that command exists for; it preserves your complete working candidate exactly and replays the two checkpoints (TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev6 and TASK-260830-2g5be6 rev3) with signing in an isolated workspace. If a regular-file conflict appears, prepare `--replay-resolutions` from the retained replay worktree's `resolution-template.json`, binding each resolution to REBASE_HEAD, its unmerged path and the SHA-256 of the explicit replacement content — keep LOGBOOK entries newest-first and never resolve by discarding trunk content.\n25|After the refresh, audit every path in `git diff --name-only a12d1bd c3aae73` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md, LOGBOOK.md and task-board.config.json all moved on trunk, so your edits must sit on top of the NEW content, never restore the old bytes, and the config must end byte-identical to the new base. Then read the command count from the candidate config rather than assuming it — it is 30 now, not 27.\n26|Everything you already wrote in the worktree is preserved and still stands: start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state in your results what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of redoing it. Your scope is unchanged.","final_result":true}}}
